{"title":"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Titu Andreescu</i>) Prove that for every <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nonnegative\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Nonnegative\">nonnegative</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/4/8e4b8ff0e9661fdceb197cefbbb0065e043f2865.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^n}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Product\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Product\">product</a> of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/2/ce2450adbcfa47f4f4fdfd4ca11121f7db81648c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> (not necessarily distinct) <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">primes</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The proof is by induction. The base is provided by the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d6fc266463b33c8b1c079a50909f3fd03ed5ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> case, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a769cbb6ddb0c8a4df4f71793099785005ff6804.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^0} + 1 = 7^1 + 1 = 2^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"169\" height=\"21\" />. To prove the inductive step, it suffices to show that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb62689de68fe62e9841cfa3c51c27b9755cc0a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 7^{2m - 1}$\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" /> for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60aac1dad8b28f806a714feb70bc7d0c8d83d2db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x^7 + 1)/(x + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"126\" height=\"19\" /> is composite. As a consequence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/d/ccd00fb50a33b0a7d397eb3e8d7b69945b688b78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^7 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" /> has at least two more prime factors than does <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43b6bcde0e66de78dde6250138abf955e45c4ed5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />. To confirm that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60aac1dad8b28f806a714feb70bc7d0c8d83d2db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x^7 + 1)/(x + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"126\" height=\"19\" /> is composite, observe that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1c115b55b6d24df93c6ac36480ed732797f60c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{x^7 + 1}{x + 1} &amp;= \\frac{(x + 1)^7 - ((x + 1)^7 - (x^7 + 1))}{x + 1} \\\\ &amp;= (x + 1)^6 - \\frac{7x(x^5 + 3x^4 + 5x^3 + 5x^2 + 3x + 1)}{x + 1} \\\\ &amp;= (x + 1)^6 - 7x(x^4 + 2x^3 + 3x^2 + 2x + 1) \\\\ &amp;= (x + 1)^6 - 7^{2m}(x^2 + x + 1)^2 \\\\ &amp;= \\{(x + 1)^3 - 7^m(x^2 + x + 1)\\}\\{(x + 1)^3 + 7^m(x^2 + x + 1)\\}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"521\" height=\"169\" />\nAlso each factor exceeds 1. It suffices to check the smaller one; <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdafd3786088681ea55e69787c2b77a33900ff0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{7x}\\leq x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"69\" height=\"19\" /> gives\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1afb10ffae70c8d0775d9ffd2d08f29414f70c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (x + 1)^3 - 7^m(x^2 + x + 1) &amp;= (x + 1)^3 - \\sqrt{7x}(x^2 + x + 1) \\\\ &amp;\\geq x^3 + 3x^2 + 3x + 1 - x(x^2 + x + 1) \\\\ &amp;= 2x^2 + 2x + 1\\geq 113 &gt; 1. \\end{align*}\" width=\"492\" height=\"74\" />\nHence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60aac1dad8b28f806a714feb70bc7d0c8d83d2db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x^7 + 1)/(x + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"126\" height=\"19\" /> is composite and the proof is complete.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We prove by induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/b/23b36a3aa7226a9b97e415ce69b584a4675876bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^n} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"19\" /> is the product of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7541e1aba5382ac9300ffe7e659794d42b7c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> (not necessarily distinct) primes.\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d6fc266463b33c8b1c079a50909f3fd03ed5ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/a/7fa4fdb05c77709e7219d928e54de9009c6b393c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^0} + 1 = 7 + 1 = 8 = 2^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"195\" height=\"21\" />, which has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45a48f105d64bc4f394bc7e66b5850ec717e7ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 = 2(0) + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> prime factors.  \nThus, the base case holds.\n</p><p>Assume that for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/c/e2c7d62172af0e738dd12f47f3f8ad8ef70cd65f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/b/23b36a3aa7226a9b97e415ce69b584a4675876bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^n} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"19\" /> is the product of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7541e1aba5382ac9300ffe7e659794d42b7c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> primes.  \nWe must show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/b/21bbcda01d79dd1a4eff2ed8d3c3048b798232eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^{n+1}} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"72\" height=\"21\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d0081a0583ea3a6edcf8e5fd5ad660b55fb52264.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> prime factors.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382b9bc055764768479f9b696101834374fe6953.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 7^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is odd, we can apply the Aurifeuillian factorization:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/f/21f7c0f8de251c23865dbdbb67d5505aa32fcae8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7a} + 1 = (7^a + 1)\\big(7^{3a} + 3\\cdot7^{2a} + 3\\cdot7^a + 1\\big)\\big(7^{\\tfrac{5a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}} + 7^{\\tfrac{3a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}} + 7^{\\tfrac{a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}}\\big)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"544\" height=\"31\" />.\n</p><p>Each factor is an integer greater than 1, so the factorization is nontrivial.\n</p><p>The first factor is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a5c44703b7d413f231d3d9d9d8678e3ee696be1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^a + 1 = 7^{7^n} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"131\" height=\"19\" />, which by the inductive hypothesis has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7541e1aba5382ac9300ffe7e659794d42b7c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> prime factors.  \nIt remains to show that the quotient\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/7/0377a5f442403b8ac853f9242ecd927b1cdeb5d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7^{7a} + 1}{7^a + 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"61\" height=\"42\" />\nis composite.\n</p><p>From the factorization above, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edc107ec34577c2cd21e4ae75943c1205c424dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7^{7a} + 1}{7^a + 1} = \\big(7^{3a} + 3\\cdot7^{2a} + 3\\cdot7^a + 1\\big)\\big(7^{\\tfrac{5a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}} + 7^{\\tfrac{3a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}} + 7^{\\tfrac{a}{2} + \\tfrac{1}{2}}\\big)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"486\" height=\"42\" />,\n</p><p>which is clearly the product of two integers greater than 1, and thus composite.\n</p><p>Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/7/0377a5f442403b8ac853f9242ecd927b1cdeb5d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7^{7a} + 1}{7^a + 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"61\" height=\"42\" /> is always composite, and hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/8/178e5bb80ddf021c129f0bf1b17ad5c6f8186aef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7a} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> has at least two more prime factors than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/934ee0ee6e564e8547846b34cc2866b98b7a4727.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^a + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>By the inductive hypothesis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/934ee0ee6e564e8547846b34cc2866b98b7a4727.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^a + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7541e1aba5382ac9300ffe7e659794d42b7c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> prime factors, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/8/178e5bb80ddf021c129f0bf1b17ad5c6f8186aef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7a} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/d/a0da73c8235b23cff6e7cfa219017211824911d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n + 3) + 2 = 2n + 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"174\" height=\"20\" /> prime factors.\n</p><p>By induction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/b/23b36a3aa7226a9b97e415ce69b584a4675876bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{7^n} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"19\" /> is the product of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7541e1aba5382ac9300ffe7e659794d42b7c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> (not necessarily distinct) primes for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  \n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>&lt;url&gt;viewtopic.php?t=145849 Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks&lt;/url&gt;</li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO\" title=\"2007 USAMO\">2007 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2007\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017214002\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.045 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.081 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 275/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1663/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8515/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   19.061      1 -total\n 39.27%    7.485      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n 30.78%    5.866      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 28.43%    5.419      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4892:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017214002 and revision id 262640. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}